Benign Breast Disease (Fibroadenoma/Fibrocystic Changes)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cyclical breast pain/lumpiness, Smooth mobile rubbery mass (fibroadenoma), Absence of skin changes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-006
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, intractable cyclical breast pain with new rapidly enlarging, tender mass causing severe distress and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of excruciating bilateral breast pain peaking tonight, complicated by severe anxiety over a newly palpable, rapidly enlarging mass. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute infectious mastitis or abscess, and to provide immediate, strong analgesia to prevent escalating distress and emergency department transfer before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My breasts are throbbing so intensely I cannot wear a shirt, and I just found a new, large lump that appeared out of nowhere today.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began gradually three days ago with cyclical, bilateral breast pain and tenderness (mastalgia) corresponding to the premenstrual phase of the menstrual cycle. Over the past 12 hours, the pain escalated sharply to an unbearable throbbing sensation. Concurrently, the patient palpated a new, distinct mass in the right breast, separate from her usual multiple bilateral nodular, rope-like fibrocystic changes. The patient reports severe hyperesthesia preventing sleep or the wearing of tight clothing. Denies systemic fever, chills, or nipple discharge.
Functional Status: Independent, currently pacing the room due to severe discomfort and anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute severe health anxiety regarding potential malignancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, holding arms away from the chest wall, in visible discomfort.
Physical Exam: Bilateral breasts exhibit diffuse nodularity consistent with fibrocystic changes. Exquisite tenderness palpated globally, worse in the upper outer quadrants. In the right upper outer quadrant, there is a distinct solitary, firm, completely smooth, highly mobile, rubbery breast mass measuring approximately 3cm, exquisitely tender to touch. There is a complete absence of skin dimpling, erythema, edema, or peau d'orange changes. No palpable axillary lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Neatly kept apartment, no immediate trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1g four hours ago with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Breast Disease (Fibrocystic exacerbation with presumed expanding macrocyst/fibroadenoma) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Mastitis - Ruled out due to complete absence of localized erythema, focal warmth, or systemic fever.

Breast Abscess - Unlikely given the lack of localized fluctuance, overlying skin inflammation, and normal temperature.

Malignant Breast Neoplasm - Unlikely due to the mass being highly mobile, perfectly smooth, and exquisitely tender, without associated skin tethering or nipple retraction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of cyclical mastalgia accompanied by a rapidly expanding, highly tender breast mass, resulting in severe functional impairment (inability to sleep or wear clothing) and profound acute anxiety. Objective clinical examination confirms a highly mobile, smooth mass without overlying inflammatory or malignant skin changes, ruling out acute mastitis or advanced carcinoma. However, the severity of the pain and distress necessitated urgent after-hours clinical evaluation and administration of potent parenteral and oral analgesia. This intervention could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without risking severe psychological distress and unnecessary, resource-intensive emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for immediate severe pain control. Prescribed oral naproxen 500mg twice daily with food. Arranged urgent formal breast ultrasound for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To consistently manage cyclical pain, apply a supportive, well-fitted sports bra immediately upon waking during the premenstrual week; experiencing the physical stabilization of the tissue will reinforce the habit of early mechanical support. To reduce baseline inflammation, completely eliminate caffeine intake during this phase; observing a distinct decrease in tissue nodularity and pain will encourage strict dietary compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to discuss ultrasound results and long-term symptom management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department immediately if the breast develops a spreading red rash, if you develop a fever greater than 38.0 C, or if the skin over the lump begins to break down or leak pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical palpation; definitive diagnosis requires ultrasound and potential fine needle aspiration.
Clinical Course: The acute pain should significantly diminish within hours following the injection. The lump is likely a cyst that filled rapidly due to hormonal changes, but must be formally imaged.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you experience spreading redness, high fevers, or localized heat suggesting a rapidly developing infection.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe pain is caused by rapid fluid accumulation within a benign cyst due to your current hormonal cycle phase. The anti-inflammatory injection will swiftly block the pain pathways, and the oral medication will maintain this relief.
Actionable Steps: Wear a supportive crop top or sports bra to bed tonight to minimize tissue movement, and strictly adhere to the naproxen schedule.

